\details{
\strong{Access.} The Genius hosts publish no robots.txt and no terms. CloudFront refuses
datacenter IPs on the LiveStats and hosted hosts: from a server set
\code{options(hoopR.proxy = ...)} or the \code{GENIUS_PROXY} environment variable to an http(s)
proxy URL (never printed or logged). Requests are spaced at least one second apart
per host; keep crawls slow and incremental. Some league sites (the NZ NBL) forbid AI
crawlers in their own robots.txt, which does not cover these hosts. Unofficial use of a
public source: payloads are not redistributed as release assets.

\strong{Org and season.} Every competition is an ORG code plus a competition id
(\code{comp_id}): \code{\link[=genius_competitions]{genius_competitions()}} lists an org's competitions with the sdv END-year
\code{season} (a START-year org lists 2025-26 as season 2026), \code{\link[=genius_match_competition]{genius_match_competition()}}
maps a game to its competition, and \code{data.json} itself names no date, competition or
season. The hosted ORG can differ from the LiveStats ORG of \verb{/u/} (NZNBL vs NZN). Match
ids are one global sequence across every Genius customer. \code{data.json} carries no global
team or player id: \code{tno} (1 or 2) and \code{pno} are in-game numbers; only finished games
are read.

\strong{Errors} (classed conditions, all inheriting \code{hoopR_error}): \code{hoopR_no_data} when
Genius has nothing for the request (HTTP 404, the S3 \code{AccessDenied} 403, an empty 200
from the hosted host, an empty recent-games window, a schedule or widget with no
matches); \code{hoopR_bad_request} for HTTP 400 / 422 or a URL that is not a Genius route
(e.g. a non-numeric id); \code{hoopR_asset_fetch} when the answer is unknown: the CloudFront
"Request blocked" 403, a rate limit or 5xx, the intermittent HTTP 500 "A website error
